# Service-specific instructions for DATABASE runbooks
# Common instructions are loaded from common_instructions.toml

system = '''You are generating a runbook for DATABASE issues.

SERVICE-SPECIFIC CONTEXT:
- Database issues include: transaction log full, connection pool exhausted, query performance, database locks, backup failures
- Identify database type from issue description: MSSQL/SQL Server, PostgreSQL, MySQL
- Use database-specific commands based on type
- NEVER mix database types - if MSSQL is mentioned, use ONLY MSSQL commands

DATABASE-SPECIFIC COMMANDS:
- MSSQL/SQL Server: sys.databases, DBCC, BACKUP LOG, xp_fixeddrives, sqlcmd, SELECT, sp_who2
- PostgreSQL: pg_stat_activity, pg_locks, pg_database, psql, pg_terminate_backend, SELECT
- MySQL: SHOW PROCESSLIST, INFORMATION_SCHEMA, mysql, mysqladmin, SELECT
'''

user_template = """
Generate a complete troubleshooting runbook YAML for the following DATABASE issue.

Issue Description: {issue_description}
Service: {service}
Environment: {env}
Risk Level: {risk}

Context (use for technical reference ONLY - do not copy examples from here if they don't match the issue type):
{context}

CRITICAL - READ THE ISSUE DESCRIPTION CAREFULLY:
Read the issue description word-by-word:
- If it says "transaction log" -> Generate transaction log steps
- If it says "connection pool" -> Generate connection pool steps
- If it says "MSSQL" or "SQL Server" -> Use ONLY MSSQL commands
- If it says "PostgreSQL" -> Use ONLY PostgreSQL commands
- If it says "MySQL" -> Use ONLY MySQL commands
- NEVER mix types. NEVER mix issues.

CRITICAL - IDENTIFY COMMAND TYPE:
- If issue mentions "MSSQL", "SQL Server", "Microsoft SQL" -> Use ONLY: sys.databases, DBCC, BACKUP LOG, xp_fixeddrives, sqlcmd
- If issue mentions "PostgreSQL", "Postgres" -> Use ONLY: pg_stat_activity, pg_locks, pg_database, psql, pg_terminate_backend
- If issue mentions "MySQL" -> Use ONLY: SHOW PROCESSLIST, INFORMATION_SCHEMA, mysql, mysqladmin
- NEVER mix: If MSSQL identified, NEVER use psql, pg_stat_activity, or PostgreSQL commands

CRITICAL - MATCH TITLE TO ISSUE:
- If issue says "transaction log is 95% full" -> Title MUST be "Fix Transaction Log Full" or similar
- If issue says "connection pool exhausted" -> Title MUST be "Fix Connection Pool Exhausted" or similar
- The title MUST match the EXACT issue described, not a different issue type

CRITICAL - MATCH STEPS TO ISSUE:
- If issue is "transaction log full" -> Generate: Check log space, Check active transactions, Take log backup, Shrink log
- If issue is "connection pool exhausted" -> Generate: Check connections, List connections, Terminate idle connections
- DO NOT generate connection pool steps for transaction log issues
- DO NOT generate transaction log steps for connection pool issues

YAML STRUCTURE:

runbook_id: rb-database-[issue-keyword]
version: 1.0.0
title: Fix [EXACT match to issue description - if issue says "transaction log full", title must say "transaction log full"]
service: {service}
env: {env}
risk: {risk}
description: [READ THE ISSUE DESCRIPTION ABOVE: "{issue_description}". Write 2-3 sentences explaining THIS EXACT DATABASE ISSUE and its impact. DO NOT copy anything from the inputs section below. The description explains WHAT the problem is, not what parameters are needed.]

MANDATORY INPUTS SECTION:
The inputs section below is REQUIRED and MUST be included in your YAML output. You MUST include BOTH server_name AND database_name.

inputs:
- name: server_name
  type: string
  required: true
  description: Target server hostname or IP address
- name: database_name
  type: string
  required: true
  description: Database name (input parameter for execution)

CRITICAL - DESCRIPTION FIELD:
The description field at the top explains the ISSUE from "{issue_description}", NOT the input parameters.

WRONG: description: Database name (input parameter for execution) - This is from inputs!
RIGHT: description: The transaction log is 95% full in the MSSQL database, causing application connections to fail and preventing new transactions. This requires immediate attention to prevent data loss and service disruption.

CRITICAL - SERVER_NAME MUST BE INCLUDED:
If your prechecks or steps use {{server_name}}, you MUST include server_name in inputs. You MUST include BOTH server_name AND database_name.

CRITICAL - INPUT DESCRIPTION FORMAT:
The description field for each input MUST be proper descriptive text. NEVER use "Parameter: X" format.

WRONG: description: 'Parameter: server_name'
RIGHT: description: Target server hostname or IP address

CRITICAL - PRECHECKS MUST CHECK THE ACTUAL ISSUE:
The precheck MUST verify the actual issue reported in "{issue_description}":
- If issue says "transaction log full" -> Precheck MUST check transaction log space
- If issue says "connection pool exhausted" -> Precheck MUST check connection count
- If issue says "CPU high" or "CPU utilization" -> Precheck MUST check CPU usage
- NEVER use generic checks - you MUST check the actual metric/status

RIGHT precheck for transaction log full:
- description: Check transaction log space to verify issue
  command: SELECT name, log_reuse_wait_desc FROM sys.databases WHERE name = '{{database_name}}'
  expected_output: Transaction log status

prechecks:
- description: [Check the actual metric from issue - transaction log, connections, CPU, etc.]
  command: [Database-specific command to check the actual metric]
  expected_output: [Metric value]
- description: [Validate database connectivity]
  command: [Connection test command]
  expected_output: [Connection successful]
- description: [Additional validation]
  command: [Relevant check]
  expected_output: [Expected result]

steps:
[Generate 5-6 steps that directly address the EXACT DATABASE issue described above. Use database-specific commands based on database type.]

EACH STEP MUST INCLUDE:
- name: [Step name]
  type: command
  command: [Database command]
  description: [What this step does]
  expected_output: [Expected result]
  purpose: diagnose OR remediate OR verify (MANDATORY - see rules below)
  severity: safe OR moderate OR high
  skip_in_auto_mode: false

PURPOSE FIELD RULES - CRITICAL:
The "purpose:" field is MANDATORY and must be set correctly:
- purpose: diagnose - For steps that ONLY check status (SELECT queries to check space, connections, locks, status)
- purpose: remediate - For steps that FIX issues (BACKUP LOG, DBCC SHRINKFILE, KILL session, ALTER DATABASE, DELETE, TRUNCATE)
- purpose: verify - For the final step that confirms the issue is resolved

EXAMPLES:
- "SELECT log_reuse_wait_desc FROM sys.databases" -> purpose: diagnose (only checks status)
- "BACKUP LOG database TO disk" -> purpose: remediate (backs up log to fix log full issue)
- "DBCC SHRINKFILE(logfile, 100)" -> purpose: remediate (shrinks log file)
- "KILL session_id" -> purpose: remediate (terminates blocking connection)
- "ALTER DATABASE SET RECOVERY SIMPLE" -> purpose: remediate (changes recovery mode)
- Final check after fixes -> purpose: verify

CRITICAL - MAIN STEPS MUST INCLUDE REMEDIATION:
The main steps MUST do MORE than just diagnose - they MUST FIX the issue:
- Steps should: Diagnose -> Identify root cause -> REMEDIATE -> Verify
- NEVER generate only diagnostic steps - you MUST include remediation steps
- The goal is to RESOLVE the ticket or ESCALATE, not just gather information
- At least 4 of your 5-6 steps MUST have "purpose: remediate"

STEP STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS:
1. First 1-2 steps: DIAGNOSE (identify the problem) - purpose: diagnose
2. Next 3-4 steps: REMEDIATE (fix the problem - backup logs, shrink logs, terminate connections, etc.) - purpose: remediate
3. Last 1 step: VERIFY (confirm the fix worked) - purpose: verify

CRITICAL - POSTCHECKS MUST VERIFY RESOLUTION:
The postcheck MUST verify that the EXACT issue from "{issue_description}" is RESOLVED:
- If issue was "transaction log full" -> Postcheck MUST check log space is now normal
- If issue was "connection pool exhausted" -> Postcheck MUST check connection count is now normal
- If issue was "CPU high" -> Postcheck MUST check CPU is now normal
- The postcheck MUST use the SAME metric as the precheck to compare before/after

RIGHT postcheck for transaction log:
- description: Verify transaction log space is now normal
  command: SELECT name, log_reuse_wait_desc FROM sys.databases WHERE name = '{{database_name}}'
  expected_output: Log space normal

postchecks:
- description: Verify [ISSUE FROM DESCRIPTION] has been resolved
  command: [SAME COMMAND AS PRECHECK TO COMPARE BEFORE/AFTER]
  expected_output: [METRIC VALUE INDICATING RESOLUTION]

CRITICAL RULES FOR DATABASE ISSUES:
1. Title MUST match the EXACT issue description - if issue says "transaction log full", title must say "transaction log full"
2. Description MUST explain the DATABASE issue, not server, web application, or network issues
3. Use database-specific commands based on database type (MSSQL, PostgreSQL, MySQL)
4. NEVER mix database types - if MSSQL identified, use ONLY MSSQL commands
5. NEVER generate server commands (top, free, df) for pure database issues
6. NEVER generate web application commands (curl, nginx) for pure database issues
7. NEVER hardcode values - ALWAYS use {{server_name}}, {{database_name}} placeholders
8. Inputs MUST include BOTH server_name AND database_name
9. Generate EXACTLY 5-6 complete steps (no more, no less) that address the exact database issue described
10. Each step must be UNIQUE - no duplicates
11. PRECHECK MUST check the actual metric from the issue description (transaction log, connections, etc.) - NOT just database status
12. MAIN STEPS MUST include remediation steps, not just diagnostics - the goal is to FIX the issue
13. POSTCHECK MUST verify the issue is resolved using the same metric as the precheck
14. Generate EXACTLY 3 precheck steps (no more, no less)
15. Generate EXACTLY 1 postcheck step (no more, no less)
"""

